From: hjviewer <haejin.cho@yonsei.ac.kr>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 22:59:31 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 7/7] hbbs: review fixes

Findings of the review of the access tickets (0006):

- A registration without a good ticket counts against an address budget
  of its own, the IP blocker's "<ip>/noticket". Old apps refused with
  NOT_DEPLOYED register again every 30 seconds, and upstream blocks an
  address for a minute once 32 registrations came less than a minute
  apart: one refused PC blocked its address every 16 minutes, ten behind
  one NAT about 40% of the time, and a PC with a ticket there got
  TOO_FREQUENT. Without the option, with MODE=off or count every
  registration goes online and the budget is the address, as before.
- A registration without a good ticket stores nothing for an ID that is
  online through a TCP/WebSocket registration with one. A client with that
  PC's uuid at its address could replace the key controllers get, which
  hjv-pk also hands to hjv-api, while the PC kept the channel. The keys of
  offline and new IDs are stored as before, so a new PC is still bound on
  its first login.
- An admitted registration keeps the public key its kid named, and is
  closed (unknown_kid) once the file puts another key under that number,
  as for a stolen key replaced in place, not only once the number goes.
- HJV_ACCESS_FILE or --hjv-access-file set but blank, as a template line
  left unfilled, is no longer taken for no option: hbbs is closed, with an
  error that says why every hour.

Tests: refused registrations from one address up to TOO_FREQUENT leave a
ticketed one from there OK; one with another key leaves hjv-pk's key of
an admitted PC; refused renewals on an admitted connection (no ticket,
another PC's) keep it past the heartbeat; a key replaced under its kid
lapses; a blank option is closed.

